1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:05,474
Most of a material's mechanical properties come from how its atoms are arranged.

2
00:00:05,474 --> 00:00:08,107
That arrangement is the crystal structure.

3
00:00:08,827 --> 00:00:13,365
Dense, regularly packed structures have lower energy than random ones.

4
00:00:13,365 --> 00:00:16,727
In a crystalline material, atoms pack in a periodic,

5
00:00:16,727 --> 00:00:19,977
three dimensional array, with long range order.

6
00:00:19,977 --> 00:00:23,731
Metals, many ceramics and some polymers are crystalline.

7
00:00:24,151 --> 00:00:28,782
A noncrystalline, or amorphous, material has no periodic packing.

8
00:00:28,782 --> 00:00:33,644
That happens with complex structures and with rapid cooling, as in glass.

9
00:00:37,344 --> 00:00:40,777
We describe crystals with the hard sphere model, where nearest

10
00:00:40,777 --> 00:00:44,731
neighbors touch, and the unit cell: the smallest repeating unit that

11
00:00:44,731 --> 00:00:48,164
still shows the crystal's chemical and physical properties.

12
00:00:48,584 --> 00:00:52,428
Translate it in every direction and it builds the whole crystal.

13
00:00:52,428 --> 00:00:57,462
Its edge length is the lattice parameter, a, just a few tenths of a nanometer.

14
00:00:57,882 --> 00:01:00,061
Three numbers describe every cell.

15
00:01:00,061 --> 00:01:02,347
N, the number of atoms per cell.

16
00:01:02,347 --> 00:01:05,961
The coordination number, the number of nearest neighbors.

17
00:01:05,961 --> 00:01:10,373
And the atomic packing factor, the fraction of the cell filled by atoms.

18
00:01:10,792 --> 00:01:14,255
To count atoms, interior atoms count fully, face atoms

19
00:01:14,255 --> 00:01:17,718
are shared by two cells, and corner atoms by eight.

20
00:01:21,418 --> 00:01:24,130
Simple cubic. One atom at each corner.

21
00:01:24,130 --> 00:01:27,468
Eight corners times one eighth, so one atom per cell.

22
00:01:27,888 --> 00:01:31,738
The atoms touch along the cube edge, so a equals two R.

23
00:01:31,738 --> 00:01:37,047
Each atom has six neighbors, and the packing factor is only zero point five two.

24
00:01:37,047 --> 00:01:39,497
Polonium is about the only example.

25
00:01:39,917 --> 00:01:44,126
Add one atom at the center of the cube, and you have body centered cubic.

26
00:01:44,126 --> 00:01:45,829
That is two atoms per cell.

27
00:01:46,249 --> 00:01:49,244
Now the atoms touch along the body diagonal.

28
00:01:49,244 --> 00:01:52,297
That diagonal is a root three, and it holds four

29
00:01:52,297 --> 00:01:55,409
radii, so a equals four R over root three.

30
00:01:55,829 --> 00:01:58,014
Each atom has eight neighbors.

31
00:01:58,014 --> 00:02:01,499
The packing factor is two times four thirds pi R cubed,

32
00:02:01,499 --> 00:02:05,339
over a cubed, which works out to zero point six eight.

33
00:02:05,339 --> 00:02:11,009
Chromium, tungsten, and iron below nine hundred twelve degrees Celsius are B C C.

34
00:02:11,429 --> 00:02:13,844
Now put an atom at every corner and in the

35
00:02:13,844 --> 00:02:16,891
center of every face: face centered cubic.

36
00:02:16,891 --> 00:02:21,951
Six faces times one half, plus one from the corners: four atoms per cell.

37
00:02:22,372 --> 00:02:25,031
The atoms touch along the face diagonal.

38
00:02:25,031 --> 00:02:28,652
a root two equals four R, so a equals two R root two.

39
00:02:29,072 --> 00:02:33,105
The coordination number is twelve, and the packing factor is zero

40
00:02:33,105 --> 00:02:37,079
point seven four, the maximum possible for equal spheres.

41
00:02:37,079 --> 00:02:43,275
Copper, nickel, gold, silver, and iron above nine hundred twelve degrees are F C C.

42
00:02:47,075 --> 00:02:50,801
The other close packed structure is hexagonal close packed.

43
00:02:50,801 --> 00:02:53,849
Twelve corner atoms count one sixth each, two face

44
00:02:53,849 --> 00:02:59,212
atoms count one half, and three atoms sit inside. That is six atoms per cell.

45
00:02:59,632 --> 00:03:03,035
Like F C C, it has a coordination number of twelve

46
00:03:03,035 --> 00:03:06,374
and a packing factor of zero point seven four.

47
00:03:06,374 --> 00:03:10,281
Magnesium, cobalt, titanium and zinc are H C P.

48
00:03:10,701 --> 00:03:13,481
So what is the difference? The stacking sequence.

49
00:03:13,481 --> 00:03:15,510
Start with a close packed layer, A.

50
00:03:15,510 --> 00:03:17,893
The next layer, B, sits in the hollows.

51
00:03:18,313 --> 00:03:20,197
The third layer decides.

52
00:03:20,197 --> 00:03:25,179
If it sits directly above A, the pattern is A B A B: that is H C P.

53
00:03:25,179 --> 00:03:31,012
If it shifts into the empty C hollows, the pattern is A B C A B C: that is F C C.

54
00:03:31,432 --> 00:03:33,780
Same packing, different order.

55
00:03:33,780 --> 00:03:38,304
And that difference is why F C C and H C P metals deform so differently.

56
00:03:42,104 --> 00:03:45,063
If you know the crystal structure, the atomic radius and

57
00:03:45,063 --> 00:03:48,022
the atomic weight, you can calculate the density.

58
00:03:48,442 --> 00:03:51,721
n is the atoms per cell, A the atomic weight, V C

59
00:03:51,721 --> 00:03:54,999
the cell volume, and N A is Avogadro's number.

60
00:03:55,420 --> 00:04:00,726
Your lecture's example: copper, F C C, with a radius of zero point one two

61
00:04:00,726 --> 00:04:05,107
eight nanometers and an atomic weight of sixty three point five.

62
00:04:05,107 --> 00:04:10,784
a is two R root two, zero point three six two nanometers, so the cell volume is four

63
00:04:10,784 --> 00:04:15,905
point seven four five times ten to the minus twenty three cubic centimeters.

64
00:04:16,325 --> 00:04:20,862
So the density is four times sixty three point five, over four point seven

65
00:04:20,862 --> 00:04:25,633
four five times ten to the minus twenty three, times Avogadro's number.

66
00:04:25,633 --> 00:04:28,308
That is eight point eight nine grams per cubic

67
00:04:28,308 --> 00:04:32,206
centimeter, close to the measured eight point nine four.

68
00:04:36,005 --> 00:04:39,971
Cubic is only one of seven crystal systems, defined by the edge

69
00:04:39,971 --> 00:04:44,469
lengths a, b and c, and the angles alpha, beta and gamma.

70
00:04:44,889 --> 00:04:48,087
Together they give fourteen Bravais lattices.

71
00:04:48,087 --> 00:04:52,575
The cubic system alone contains simple cubic, B C C and F C C.

72
00:04:52,575 --> 00:04:55,896
In this course we focus on cubic and hexagonal.

73
00:04:59,596 --> 00:05:04,303
Finally, how do we name directions and planes? With Miller indices.

74
00:05:04,303 --> 00:05:09,674
Slip, the way metals deform, happens on specific planes along specific directions.

75
00:05:10,095 --> 00:05:13,824
For a direction, draw the vector from the origin, read its

76
00:05:13,824 --> 00:05:17,665
projections on x, y and z in units of the lattice parameter,

77
00:05:17,665 --> 00:05:21,846
clear the fractions, and write the integers in square brackets.

78
00:05:21,846 --> 00:05:24,501
No commas, and a bar over negatives.

79
00:05:24,920 --> 00:05:28,442
So the cube edge is one zero zero, the face diagonal is

80
00:05:28,442 --> 00:05:32,144
one one zero, and the body diagonal is one one one.

81
00:05:32,144 --> 00:05:37,995
In a cubic crystal, equivalent directions form a family, written in angle brackets.

82
00:05:38,415 --> 00:05:43,214
For a plane, find where it cuts the axes, take reciprocals, with one over

83
00:05:43,214 --> 00:05:47,674
infinity equal to zero, clear fractions, and use round brackets.

84
00:05:47,674 --> 00:05:51,796
If the plane passes through the origin, move the origin first.

85
00:05:52,216 --> 00:05:57,246
A plane cutting x at one and parallel to y and z is the one zero zero.

86
00:05:57,246 --> 00:06:00,512
Cutting x and y at one is the one one zero.

87
00:06:00,512 --> 00:06:03,844
Cutting all three at one is the one one one.

88
00:06:04,264 --> 00:06:06,930
Curly brackets denote a family of planes.

89
00:06:06,930 --> 00:06:11,429
And when properties change with direction, the crystal is anisotropic.

90
00:06:11,429 --> 00:06:13,929
When they do not, it is isotropic.

91
00:06:17,729 --> 00:06:19,640
Here is the summary of topic three.

92
00:06:20,060 --> 00:06:26,523
Simple cubic, B C C and F C C: one, two and four atoms, coordination six, eight and

93
00:06:26,523 --> 00:06:32,735
twelve, packing zero point five two, zero point six eight and zero point seven four.

94
00:06:33,155 --> 00:06:39,005
H C P matches F C C in packing, but stacks A B A B instead of A B C A B C.

95
00:06:39,425 --> 00:06:41,685
Density is n A over V C N A.

96
00:06:42,105 --> 00:06:46,933
And Miller indices name directions in square brackets and planes in round ones.

97
00:06:46,933 --> 00:06:51,985
Next, we count atoms on those lines and planes, and use X rays to see them.
